content.text:
Candidate design.

Usage stays feature owned.

```svelte
<div class="summary-header">
  <DeclaredTotals totals={summary.declaredTotals} {period} />
  <PeriodSelector {period} />
</div>
<ContentChange changeKey={period}>
  <!-- Existing secondary content -->
  <SignedBarList variant="compact" {bars} {baseline} changeKey={period} />
</ContentChange>
```

`DeclaredTotals` passes `changeKey={period}` to `Metric`. Both member and funder summaries use this arrangement. Keep the metric outside the generic fade so the roll has one motion owner.

Layout belongs to the two summary features. Use grid columns `minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, auto)`, token gaps, and top alignment. Give the totals child `min-width: 0`. Keep filters full width within their column and right aligned. Collapse to one column at the existing mobile breakpoint. This removes the empty desktop row while preserving native links, summary placement, and horizontal scrolling for exact oversized amounts. Do not change IdentityHeader or the Summary/history navigation.

Public interfaces.

```ts
// Metric.svelte
changeKey?: string;

// SignedBarList.svelte
changeKey?: string;
```

Omitting the key retains the existing static metric/bar behavior. A provided key enables replay on committed changes, with no initial metric or summary bar animation. Period remains a feature type; shared UI receives an opaque string.

Metric should animate a persistent inner span inside the existing `.member-total` region. The exact new formatted string enters from below with `translateY(100%)` to zero, using a clipped inner viewport. Never transform the keyboard-scrollable region itself. Keep its tabindex, focus outline, horizontal overflow, region label and title. There is only one accessible value. Unavailable text stays ordinary text and should not roll.

Use an internal motion action/controller with this shape.

```ts
type EntryMotion = "roll-up" | "row" | "bar";
type MotionUpdate = {
  changeKey?: string;
  motion: EntryMotion;
  initial?: boolean;
  index?: number;
  origin?: "left" | "right";
};

function entryMotion(
  node: HTMLElement,
  initial: MotionUpdate,
): {
  update(next: MotionUpdate): void;
  destroy(): void;
};
```

The helper owns current key, active animation, reduced-motion listener and cleanup. Ignore the first key. On changed committed keys, cancel the previous animation, then animate the updated existing element. Reduced motion immediately cancels active work and suppresses subsequent animations. Update the remembered key even while reduced motion is active, so re-enabling motion does not replay an old change. No frame counters or rendered financial intermediate values.

Put canonical animation recipes and timings in the helper. Row entry matches rankings with opacity zero and `translateY(8px)` to rest over 300ms. Bar entry matches rankings with `scaleX(0)` to one over 420ms. Both use 35ms row staggering. The metric roll uses the existing 200ms content-change duration and ease-out.

SignedBarList applies row motion to each persistent `li` and bar motion to its decorative span. Positive bars use left origin. Negative bars use right origin because their existing geometry extends left from `--baseline`. Leave baseline position, widths, signs and exact labels under existing ownership.

Migrate RankingList to the same helper with `initial: true`. This preserves its initial entry behavior and avoids copying animation definitions into SignedBarList. Remove its equivalent local animation declarations and keyframes. RankingList does not need a new public replay prop for this request. Its current parent ContentChange remains unchanged.

Alternative screened.

A configurable ContentChange variant such as `"roll-up"` or `"rankings"` looks smaller initially but cannot correctly target the metric string versus signed bars without descendant selectors or caller-authored animation targets. It would couple generic composition to component internals and make signed origins a wrapper concern. Reject it.

Keeping ranking CSS unchanged and duplicating its frames in SignedBarList is another small diff, but leaves two definitions of the same visual language. Prefer the internal shared helper.

File scope.

- `MemberSummary.svelte`, `FunderSummary.svelte` own compact header arrangement.
- `DeclaredTotals.svelte` forwards committed period to Metric.
- `Metric.svelte` owns the roll viewport and persistent value span.
- `SignedBarList.svelte` owns row/bar attachment and sign origins.
- `RankingList.svelte` consumes the shared entry recipes.
- One internal motion module owns lifecycle and recipes.
- UI README, composition recipe and gallery document the new optional change key.
- Design-system and periods Playwright tests cover behavior.

Verification should assert desktop header overlap in vertical position and 320px page containment; native period focus survives slow navigation; exact large decimals remain unchanged and keyboard scrollable; no summary motion on initial render or pending requests; committed changes produce upward roll and ranking-equivalent frames/timings; negative bar origin is right; rapid changes cancel previous motion; live reduced-motion changes cancel active motion; existing disclosure state and metric node identity survive.

Concern to check during implementation. RankingList currently starts CSS animation immediately when mounted, including before hydration. Moving its initial animation into a browser action starts it at hydration instead. If preserving that exact pre-hydration timing is part of “initial rendering contract,” retain its CSS implementation and share recipes through a common UI animation stylesheet instead. The summary contract remains static initially in either design.
